-  <message name="IDS_SETTINGS_PRIVACY_SANDBOX_LEARN_MORE_DIALOG_TOPICS_DATA_MANAGEMENT" desc="TOPICS API Bullet 3 of 3 that appear beneath the â€œYour interests as estimated by Chromeâ€ subtitle. We break the description into the 3 pieces users most care about: 1) what data, 2) why this data, 3) what can I do about it? * â€œHow you can manage your data:â€ is bold and serves as a label to help people skimming the page. * â€œTo protect your privacyâ€: Today, most data is kept for long periods of time. This new Topics API doesnâ€™t retain estimated topics for more than 3 weeks. The only reason for this feature is to protect the userâ€™s privacy. * â€œautoâ€: isnâ€™t strictly necessary, but it conveys that this feature is an ongoing part of the service. This isnâ€™t describing, for example, a one-time deletion. * â€œolder than 4 weeksâ€: Weâ€™re trying to convey a rolling delete. Imagine a user opens a browser for the very first time. As the user browses, Chrome will estimate up to 5 topics of interest in the first week. In the second week, Chrome will estimate up to 5 additional topics of interest. The same for the third week, for a total of 15 potential interests. In week 4, Chrome will automatically delete the 5 interests from week 1 so that there arenâ€™t ever more than 15 topics associated with a user at any given time. * â€œinterests can refreshâ€: If a user loves horror films and reads about them for months on end, the interest â€œHorror moviesâ€ might get deleted and refreshed in the same week, so that â€œHorror moviesâ€ doesnâ€™t ever disappear from the userâ€™s list of interests. * â€œOr you canâ€¦â€: A user might love horror movies but canâ€™t stand the idea of seeing ads about horror movies (because even the visuals will ruin the surprise of opening night). In this case, the user can remove â€œHorror moviesâ€ from the list of interests Chrome will consider. This removal remains in place indefinitely. Note that the user might still see ads about Horror movies from different ad networks or by other means than Chromeâ€™s â€œBrowser-based ad personalizationâ€ setting.">
